 toc:{wear:'What to wear for padel',men:'Men: shirts, shorts and socks',women:'Women: skirts, dresses, tops and shorts',fabric:'Fabric, fit and ball pockets',brands:'What brand lines usually mean',weather:'Cold and wet UK clubs',compare:'What we will compare later',faq:'FAQ'},
 sections:[
 {id:'wear',h:'What to wear for padel',html:`<p>Padel is played in pairs on an enclosed court, so you change direction often and bend for balls that come off the glass. Clothing should stretch at the shoulders and hips, wick sweat, and stay put when you sprint to the net. There is no formal dress code at most UK clubs. Soft-soled non-marking shoes are the one hard requirement; see our <a href="/best-padel-shoes/">padel shoes</a> guides for men and women.</p><p>Start with a short-sleeve top, shorts or a skirt, and sports socks. Add a light layer you can peel off between games if the club is cool. Avoid jeans, cotton hoodies that soak up sweat, and shoes that leave black marks on the court.</p>`},
 {id:'men',h:'Men: shirts, shorts and socks',html:`<p>A fitted athletic shirt with mesh panels keeps you cooler than a loose cotton tee. Look for a hem that stays tucked or long enough that it does not ride up on overheads. Shorts need a secure waistband and enough length to cover the upper thigh when you lunge; many players prefer a zip or button pocket for a spare overgrip or keys.</p><p>Padel-specific socks are usually mid-crew with cushioning under the ball of the foot and a snug arch. That combination reduces slip inside the shoe during side steps. If you already own tennis socks with a similar build, they work well. Pair shorts with <a href="/mens-padel-shoes/">men's padel shoes</a> that match your usual outsole preference.</p>`},
 {id:'women',h:'Women: skirts, dresses, tops and shorts',html:`<p>Skirts and sleeveless dresses with built-in shorts are popular because the ball sits in a side pocket and the shorts stop the skirt riding up. Tops range from fitted tanks to short-sleeve shirts; choose a cut that does not gap when you reach for a bandeja. Shorts alone are equally common, especially for players who come from tennis or running.</p><p>Check the pocket depth before you buy: a shallow pocket drops the ball when you sprint. Built-in shorts should sit flat at the waist so they do not roll. For shoe pairing, see <a href="/womens-padel-shoes/">women's padel shoes</a>.</p>`},
 {id:'fabric',h:'Fabric, fit and ball pockets',html:`<p>Polyester blends with elastane are the usual choice: they dry quickly and stretch. Flat seams reduce chafing on long sessions. A slightly compressive fit at the torso helps a shirt stay in place; leave enough room at the shoulders for a full swing.</p><p>Ball storage is the detail players notice on court. Options include a side skirt pocket, a small zip on shorts, or tucking the ball into the waistband. Test the pocket with a real padel ball if you can: if it falls out when you crouch, it will fall out in a rally. Dark colours hide court dust; light colours show sweat less on camera but show dirt sooner.</p>`},
 {id:'brands',h:'What brand lines usually mean',html:`<p>Bullpadel, Adidas, Head and Nox all sell clothing ranges that match their racket collections. The brand name does not change how fabric behaves; it mainly decides cut, colourways and whether the line includes skirts, dresses or only shirts and shorts. Club teams often pick one brand so shirts can be personalised with a name and number.</p><p>If you already like a brand's rackets or shoes, buying the matching kit is convenient, not mandatory. Fit and fabric come first. Browse <a href="/brands/bullpadel/">Bullpadel</a> and other brand hubs if you want to see how their gear ranges sit together, then judge clothing on the hang of the garment itself.</p>`},
 {id:'weather',h:'Cold and wet UK clubs',html:`<p>Many UK clubs are indoors but poorly heated, or outdoors with wind between the glass. A light mid-layer you can remove after the warm-up is more useful than a heavy hoodie. Avoid cotton next to the skin: once wet it stays cold. In outdoor venues, a compact rain shell in the <a href="/bags/">padel bag</a> helps between matches; you will not play in a waterproof, but you will walk to the court in one.</p><p>In summer, pale colours and mesh panels matter more than brand. Carry a dry shirt if you play back-to-back matches. Socks that wick well matter as much as the shirt when courts run warm.</p>`},
 {id:'compare',h:'What we will compare later',html:`<p>When we list padel clothing side by side, this section will become a comparison table. The columns we plan to use are garment type (shirt, skirt, dress, shorts, socks), gender cut, fabric notes and whether there is a ball pocket. Until that catalogue is ready, use the advice above and the shoe and bag links to complete a kit.</p>`},
 ],
 faq:[
  ['Is there a dress code for padel in the UK?','Most clubs only ask for non-marking court shoes and normal sports clothing. Check your club if you have a league that sets a kit colour.'],
  ['Do I need padel-branded clothes?','No. Any breathable, stretchy sports kit that lets you lunge and reach will work. Brand lines mainly add cuts and colours aimed at padel.'],
  ['Skirt or shorts for padel?','Either. Skirts and dresses with built-in shorts and a ball pocket are popular; shorts suit players who prefer a freer hip.'],
  ['What socks work for padel?','Mid-crew sports socks with cushioning under the forefoot and a snug arch reduce slip inside the shoe on side steps.'],
  ['Can I wear tennis clothes for padel?','Yes, in most clubs. The movements are similar enough that tennis shirts, skirts and shorts transfer well.'],
  ['What should I wear in a cold club?','Start with a light mid-layer over a wicking shirt and take it off after the warm-up. Avoid cotton against the skin.'],
 ],
